 "registered_design": {
  "basis_note_claude": "the MODEL-level intrinsic directions (analytic_directions over all reachable columns, not the TR0+TS0-restricted ones), restricted to each table's shared support and orthonormalised; their exactness is checked on the 23,741 generating-set rows (max |G g| <= 1e-9 before restriction)",
  "canonicaliser_user": "canonicalise toward Turner 2004 along the 2 intrinsic directions: g* = argmin ||delta + g|| over their span on the shared support; report ||delta|| vs ||delta + g*||, and per table the rms per-entry difference before and after for the six tables: how much of the apparent per-entry difference goes away. Langdon is the case of interest; Andronescu and Turner 1999 alongside.",
  "control": "per-table constant-shift control: keep each of the six tables' |mean shift|, flip the signs; all 2^6 = 64 sign patterns enumerated exactly; the statistic is the intrinsic fraction of the per-table-constant part of delta; report the true pattern's rank among the 64 and the per-table mean shifts themselves (kcal/mol)",
  "execution": "scripts/turner_typeb_langdon_gauge.py, its own artifact (runs/2026-10-12_turner_typeb_langdon_gauge), one core, while the fold runs; it never writes the Type B artifact",
  "status": "registered after seeing Experiment 1; EXPLORATORY whatever it returns",
  "structure_note_claude": "the 2 intrinsic directions are per-table constant shifts (N1: +1 on every mismatchH entry, -1 on every hairpin[3..30]; N2: +1 on every mismatchI, mismatch1nI, mismatch23I entry, -2 on every internal_loop[4..30]), so delta's intrinsic component is set entirely by delta's per-table means over those six tables. Any revision that shifts those tables by constants projects onto them, whether or not the shifts satisfy the gauge relations.",
  "why_user": "if it holds, the paper's most concrete Type A example: a published table sitting 3.8 kcal/mol along an exact gauge direction; and the natural test of the canonicaliser"
